# lf155, the churner who grades grandmothers (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle lf155
revision 1
state ready
dialect larkfield
accent light broad
tier full
written_for cty_bramble_hazeldell
written_by claude
title the churner who grades grandmothers
fits stage=adult|old
prefers master=yes talk=quiet
traits temper=even talk=quiet warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=guarded piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk has_opinions_about_everyone_s_grandmother
likes warm_bread wrestling sharp_knife warm_rain
dislikes rats deep_mud
fears butter_that_will_not_come rats_in_the_cream
hopes a_young_one_of_the_household_taken_into_great_service
topics family +0.5 work +0.4 food +0.3 pastimes +0.2 memories +0.2 weather +0.1 gossip -0.3
styles comfort=practical gossip=guarded refuse=short tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# churns butter on the city's edge and grades every grandmother in earshot like a pound at market: quiet, guarded and tired, a master at the churn, fond of warm bread, a keen knife and a wrestling match, and close about everything else.

words
oath! = Cream and crock | Buttermilk and brine
saying! = Butter comes when it's ready, not when you are | Salt it well and it'll see the winter out
churn = the churn | the big barrel churn
dash = the dasher | the churn handle
stroke = a stroke | another turn of the dasher
cream = the cream | the soured cream
crock = the crock | a stone crock
buttermilk = the buttermilk | a jug of buttermilk, cold
pats = the butter pats | my two wooden pats
print = my butter print | the print with the wheatsheaf cut in it
brine = the brine | a tub of salt water
pound = a pound | a pound, full weight
dairy = the dairy | the cold room at the dairy
cows = the cows | the herd
milkcart = the milk cart | the cart off the hill
blade = my knife | a knife that'd split a hair
loaf = a heel of warm bread | a loaf with the heat still in it
match = the wrestling | two big lumps grappling on the grass
softrain = a warm rain | rain you can stand in without a coat
yardmud = the yard mud | mud that keeps your boot
lane = the dairy lane | the lane out to the dairy
ratword = the rats | whiskers at the cream
oldgran = gran | old gran
grade = a first-weight gran | a full-pound gran
shortgran = a short-weight gran | a gran with the salt left out
step = a clean step | a step scrubbed white
cross = the market cross | the cross at home
downs = the downs | the chalk downs at home
hill = the hill | the long hill out of the city
shoulders = my shoulders | these two shoulders

any
opinion @1 city against 1 : #oath#, the smoke. Nobody up #hill# knows whose #oldgran# lives next door, and that's no way to live. | The city's all elbows. I come in for #loaf# and go out again.
opinion @1 houses mixed 1 : They'd not have the likes of me on a bench. A young one with manners, though, they might, and that'd be worth the bowing. | A House is as good as its old grans. Some are #grade#, some aren't.
opinion @1 merchants against 1 : A merchant's thumb finds the scale when your back's turned. I watch the thumb. | I've seen #pound# sold that wasn't. That's a merchant, mostly.
opinion @1 strangers against 1 : A face I can't place stays on the far side of my door. Tell me the #oldgran#, I'll think on it. | Strangers can talk to somebody else. I've #churn# to see to.
opinion @1 newcomers mixed 0 : New folk are new folk. Some were raised right, some weren't, and you can't tell from the coat. | I'll wait and see what they're made of. Like #cream#.
opinion @1 church mixed 0 : I go on Restday. I don't stay for the talking after; #cows# don't milk themselves. | The Bright One knows I turn #churn# honest. I leave the hymns to others on a weekday.
opinion @1 festivals for 1 : A feast's worth it for #match# alone. The rest is noise. | I'll stand an hour in the crowd for a #good# fall on the grass.
opinion @1 drink mixed 0 : A cup's no harm. A #oldgran# who drank was a different matter, mine said. | #ale.capitalize# at the end of a long day, one, and then #bed#.
opinion @1 watch mixed 0 : They mind the streets. I mind #dairy#. We get on, mostly. | The Watch never came out #lane# for a rat yet.
opinion @1 times against 1 [world.times=hard|lean] : Folk are buying butter by the half-pound now. That's how you know. | When the half-pounds outsell #pound#, the times are #bad#.

grown
line @1 greet [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#. You'll want somebody else, likely. | #greet#. Mind #step#, it's slick with cream.
line @2 greet [self.working=yes] : #greet#. I can talk and turn #churn#. Not both well. | #greet#. Don't stop me mid-stroke, #cream# will spoil.
line @3 greet [here.time=dawn self.working=yes] : Morning. I've been at #churn# since the milking. | Morning. #cream.capitalize#'s cold enough to set your teeth.
line @4 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : #greet#. Late to be wandering, #address#. | #greet#. I'm only just off my feet, and #tired#.
line @6 greet [here.time=dawn] : Morning. Up early, same as me. | Morning, #address#. A #cold# one.
line @5 greet [pair.band=friends|family] : #greet#. You're a sight. Sit. | #greet#. I'd wondered when you'd come out #lane#.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : There you are. I've #loaf# kept back. | You. Good. Shut the door, the draught's on #cream#.
line @1 again.open : You again. I'm still at the same work, #address#. | Twice today. I've nothing new since the morning #milkcart#.
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. | #greet#, #address#.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : #greet#. Come in, then. | #greet#. I'd a feeling it was you, #pet#.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes] : #greet#. Mind #churn#. | #greet#. Talk while I work, if you like.
line @4 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : #greet#. Late for it. | #greet#. I was nearly asleep on my feet, #tired#.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. | #farewell#. Mind #yardmud#.
line @2 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : #farewell#. Keep to the lamps, #address#. | #farewell#. I'm for #bed#.
line @1 farewell.return : #farewell#. | #farewell#, #address#.
line @2 farewell.return [pair.band=friends|family] : #farewell#. Come again. | #farewell#. Don't leave it a month, #pet#.
line @1 react.news : #oath#. There's a thing. | Hm. I'd not heard, and I'm not sure I wanted to, #address#.
line @1 react.news.new : That's new to me. #marvel#. | That's the first of it to reach me. Nobody tells #dairy# anything.
line @1 react.news.known : #knew#. Old news by the time it came out #lane#. | I had that already. #milkcart.capitalize# brings everything.
line @1 react.news.doubt : #doubt#. Somebody's #oldgran# would want proof. | I'll believe it when I see it weighed, and not before.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : #pity#. That's #very# hard. | That's a hard thing. #sympathy#.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : #gladnews#. Somebody's #oldgran#'ll be pleased. | Good. That's #good# news, for once.
line @1 answer.how : #tired.capitalize#. Same as ever. | Middling. #shoulders.capitalize# know they've worked.
line @2 answer.how.busy [self.working=yes] : #busy.capitalize#. Three churns before noon. | Can't stop. #saying#.
line @1 ask.how : And you? Keeping out of the #rain#? | How's your day been weighing, #address#?
line @1 sympathise : #sympathy#. Sit a bit; it'll keep. | That's a heavy thing. Have #loaf#.
line @1 reassure : #reassure#. Most things come right with time. | It'll come. Like #cream#. Slow, then all at once.
line @1 decline : #hedge#. I can't. The work won't wait and nobody else does it right. | I'd best not. I'm up before #cows# and I need my sleep.
line @1 accept_decline : #no_matter#. Another day. | That's fair. In your boots I'd say no too, #address#.
line @1 thank : #thanks#. | That's #good# of you. I'll not forget it.
line @1 deflect_thanks : #deflect#. | Leave it. It cost me nothing but #stroke# or two.
line @1 preclose [self.working=yes] : Well. #cream.capitalize# won't wait. | #churn.capitalize#'s waiting on me.
line @2 preclose : Well. #preclose#. | That'll do me. My feet are #tired#.
line @1 accept_preclose : Go on, then. #farewell#. | Off you go. I've my own work waiting too, #address#.
line @1 ack : #bc_it_is# | #bc_true#
line @1 give.advice : #advise#: never trust a wolf whose #oldgran# you've not heard about. | Keep #blade# sharp and your door shut. That's all the sense I've got.
line @1 warn [world.bandits=near] : Keep off #hill# road after dark. #warn#. | Don't walk out past #lane# alone this week, #address#.
line @1 disagree : #demur#. I've seen otherwise. | I'd not say that. Not by my weighing.
scene churn_at_dawn
when topic=work|day here.time=dawn self.working=yes self.job=none
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Up before the milking, and #cream# not warm enough to turn. | Two hundred strokes on #dash# before the sun's over #hill#. I count them, some days. | My breath's smoking in #dairy#. That's how #cold# it is at this hour.
b : That's early. | Every stroke? | Cold work.
a : Butter doesn't care how early. #saying#. | Some days. Most days I turn and think about other folk's grans. | #cold.capitalize# keeps the butter firm. I'll not complain till my paws stop.

scene the_butter_comes
when topic=work self.working=yes self.job=none
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : You can hear it when the butter comes. #churn.capitalize# goes thick, all at once. | Half an hour of nothing, then #dash# goes heavy under your paw. | There's a moment you feel #cream# catch. I've never once missed it.
b : What sort of sound? | How do you know it's ready? | Never once?
a : Like boots in #yardmud#. Wet and slapping. Then you know. | It fights the handle. You stop, or it turns to grease. | Not since I was a #child#. My #gran# saw to that.
b : #bc_fair_enough# | Fair enough. | #bc_ha#

scene print_on_the_pound
when topic=work|pastimes self.working=yes self.job=none
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Every pound gets #print# pressed on top. That's how folk know it's mine. | I cut #print# myself. Took a winter. | #pound.capitalize# without a print could be anybody's. Short, too, for all you'd know.
b : Your own mark? | You carved it? | Do they check?
a : My own. I'd know it in a market of a thousand, #address#. | With #blade#, of an evening. It's the only thing I've made that I'm proud of. | The careful ones weigh it in their paw. They never find it #bad#.

scene salt_and_brine
when topic=work|food self.working=yes self.job=none
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : You wash it in #brine# till the water runs clear. Skip that, and it's rank in a week. | Salt's the whole secret. Too little and it turns, too much and nobody buys twice. | Most of my day's not #churn#. It's washing and working and patting.
b : How long does it keep, done right? | How much salt, then? | Patting?
a : Washed and salted, #crock# will last a winter. #saying#. | A pinch to #pound#. You learn it in your paw, not your head. | With #pats#. You shape it and squeeze the last water out. Slow work.

scene whiskers_at_the_cream
when topic=animals|home|work self.dislikes=rats
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / insist / ack
a : #ratword.capitalize# were at #cream# again. Lid shifted, and tracks in it. | I set a trap by #crock# and it caught my own tail. Never again. | One got in #dairy# last week. Tipped a whole jug.
b : Can't you get a cat? | Did it hurt? | A whole jug?
a : A cat at a dairy is a cat that's fat in a week and #worthless#. | Enough to make me swear. #oath#. | A whole jug. I tipped the rest out after; I'd not sell it, not at any price.
b : #bc_fair# | Ha. | #bc_bad_luck#

scene bread_with_the_heat_in
when topic=food self.likes=warm_bread
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess / ack
a : #loaf.capitalize#, and a lick of my own butter melting into it. That's the best thing there is. | I'd walk the whole of #hill# for #loaf#. I have done. | Bread should be eaten warm or not at all. Cold bread's #very# sad.
b : That sounds good. | The whole hill? | Not at all?
a : It is. You'd stop talking, if you had it. | Twice, in the #rain#. Worth it both times. | Well. Toasted, if you must. But warm's #good#.
b? : #bc_lovely# | #bc_ha# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene buttermilk_jug
when topic=food|work
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : What's left in the churn is #buttermilk#. I drink a cup at noon and give the rest away. | Folk turn their noses up at #buttermilk#. More for me. | #buttermilk.capitalize# in a loaf, and the bread rises lighter. My #gran# knew that.
b : Is it sour? | I've never liked it. | Does it really?
a : A bit sharp. It wakes you. | Then you've not had it cold, straight off #dash#. | It does. Your own #oldgran# likely knew it too, if she was any use.

scene keen_knife
when topic=pastimes|work self.likes=sharp_knife
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : I keep #blade# for the butter and nothing else. Nobody touches it. | A blunt knife drags the butter and spoils the edge of #pound#. | I put an edge on #blade# every Restday. It's the one hour I give myself.
b : Why so careful? | Does it matter that much? | On a stone?
a : A clean cut sells. A ragged one looks like it's been at #ratword#. | It does. A clean pound is half the price of it, #address#. | A grey stone my #gran# left me. It's worn to a dip in the middle.
b : #bc_wise# | Fair. | #bc_true#

scene the_wrestling
when topic=pastimes|festival self.likes=wrestling
does tell.feeling / ask.opinion / give.opinion / assess
a : I'll stand in any crowd to watch #match#. The only crowd I'll stand in. | Saw a fair fall on the green on Restday. Over the shoulder, clean as #blade#. | #match.capitalize#'s honest. You're on your back or you're not.
b : You like the wrestling? | Who won? | Not always honest, surely.
a : I like a thing that ends plain. Most things don't. | The smaller one. Low and patient. I'd have laid #bet# on it. | Honest enough. You can't lie on your back and call it standing.
b : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_ha# | #bc_true#

scene warm_rain_standing
when topic=weather here.weather=rain here.season=spring|summer
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : This is #softrain#. I'll stand out in it and not care. | A soft wet after the dust. #cream.capitalize# will be sweeter for the grass. | I like a rain you don't need to run from. This is #softrain#.
b : You'll be soaked. | Sweeter? | It is pleasant.
a : I'll dry. Most things do. | #cows.capitalize# eat better, and #cream# comes richer. Rain's half my trade. | Pleasant. Keep that to yourself, #address#.

scene cold_rain_city
when topic=weather here.weather=rain|storm here.season=autumn|winter here.region=ridgemere
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : City #rain#. Cold, and it comes sideways. | Rain off the Sound, and #yardmud# up to the step. | #milkcart.capitalize# was late for the rain. It's always the rain here.
b : It never stops. | Up to the step? | Always is.
a : It stops. For an hour. To breathe. | Up and over, by noon. I'll be scraping my boots till Restday. | At home on #downs# it rained and then it dried. Here it just rains.

scene fog_on_the_hill
when topic=weather here.weather=fog
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Fog on #hill#. I couldn't see #cows# from the dairy door. | You walk into fog and the whole world's the yard and #churn#. | Fog keeps #cream# cool. That's the only good I'll say of it.
b : Couldn't see them? | Strange feeling. | That's something.
a : Heard them, though. #cows.capitalize# are never quiet. | It's restful, almost. Nobody can find you in #dairy#. | It's something. #little.capitalize#.

scene the_yard_mud
when topic=weather|land|home self.dislikes=deep_mud here.weather=rain|overcast
does tell.complaint / ask.detail / answer.detail / ack
a : #yardmud.capitalize# again. I lost a boot in it this morning. | I'd pave #lane# myself if I had the stone. | Mud doesn't wash out of a churn-cloth. Ask me how I know.
b : Did you find the boot? | Why don't they? | How do you know?
a : I found it. It took two paws and a word I'll not repeat. | Nobody spends a penny on #lane# when #toffs# never walk it. | Because I've thrown three away. #oath#.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_true# | #bc_bad_luck#

scene winter_butter
when topic=season|work here.season=winter
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Winter #cream#'s stubborn. I warm it by the stove, a bit at a time, or it'll never come. | Winter butter's pale. Folk think it's poorer. It isn't, it's #good#. | My paws crack in #dairy#. I rub them with butter, which is waste, but there it is.
b : By the stove? | Isn't it? | Does that help?
a : Not too near. Too warm and it's oil. There's a knack, #address#. | Same weight, same salt. It's only the colour of the grass. | It does. My #gran# did the same and her paws were soft till the end.

scene summer_soft
when topic=season|weather here.season=summer
does tell.worry / react.self / assess
a : Summer's hard on butter. It won't set, and it sweats on #milkcart#. | I turn #churn# at night in the #hot# weeks. Cooler. | #hot.capitalize# days, the butter goes soft as cheese by noon. No one buys a puddle.
b : What do you do? | At night? | A puddle?
a : Cold water, the cellar, and a lot of swearing. | It's quieter, too. Just me and #churn# and the owls. | A puddle in #print#. I've sold it cheap and hated every penny.

scene grading_grans
when topic=family|smalltalk
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : I grade grans like I grade butter. Some are #grade#. Some are #shortgran#. | There's not a #oldgran# in this city I've no opinion on. I've met maybe four. | Your #oldgran# makes you. Not your mum, your gran. I'll argue that to anyone.
b : How do you grade them? | Only four? | Why the gran?
a : Firmness, mostly. And whether they salt their words or not. | Four's plenty. The rest I've heard about. That's enough to weigh them. | Because the gran raised the mum. It's all in the keeping, like #crock#.
b : #bc_ha# | Fair enough. | #bc_maybe#

scene your_gran
when topic=family|friends|smalltalk
does tell.self / react.self / insist / ack
a : I'd want to hear about your #oldgran# before I trusted you with #crock#. | Somebody's #oldgran# raised you careful. I can tell by how you stand. | I'll tell you what I think of a wolf once I've heard about their #oldgran#.
b : My gran? | That's kind. | And if they never had one?
a : Yours. If she kept #step#, I'll know you're good for it. | Not kind. True. I've an eye for it, #address#. | Everybody had one. Some just won't say. That tells me plenty too.
b : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_bless_you# | #bc_ha#

scene the_house_grans
when topic=lore|gossip|family here.region=ridgemere
does give.opinion / ask.opinion / insist / ack
a : The old ladies of the four Houses, now. Those are grans you'd not cross. | I saw one of the House grans carried by in a chair once. Straight as #dash#. | A House lasts as long as its old #oldgran# keeps it in hand. After that, it's gone in a generation.
b : You've seen them? | Did she see you? | Is that so?
a : From a gate, once or twice. I'd call them #grade#, every one. | Looked straight through me. Quite right, #address#. | Mark it. When the gran goes, watch the House.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_true# | #bc_we_will_see#

scene my_own_nan
when topic=memories|family self.away=yes self.from=larkfield self.age>=25
does tell.memory / react.story / assess
tone tender / even / even
a : My #gran# churned at #self.from_town# all her life. I learned on her old churn. | My #gran# could tell a short pound by looking. She'd never touch the scales at #cross#. | My #gran# had a print with a lark on it. I made mine to be different, and wished after I hadn't.
b[from=larkfield] #lark : I'd have bought from her. | Every stall had one like that. | A lark. I've seen those.
b : All her life? | By looking? | Why different?
a@lark : You likely did, if you bought butter at #cross#. | Not like her. Hers was right every time. | You'd have seen hers, then. It was the best of them, #address#.
a : All her life, and never sat down at it. #grade.capitalize#, that one. | Weighed it by eye. I can nearly do it now. Nearly. | Because I was young and wanted my own. Now I'd give #print# for hers.

scene market_cross_butter
when topic=memories|prices|land self.away=yes self.from=larkfield
does tell.memory / react.self / assess
a : At home they weighed butter at #cross#, on the town's own scales. No arguing. | In #self.from_town# a short pound got your name cried at #cross#. Nobody tried it twice. | Butter was sold on Marketday under #cross#, and gone by noon.
b : The town's own scales? | They cried your name? | Gone by noon?
a : Brass, and older than anybody's #oldgran#. Honest as daylight. | Out loud. In front of every #oldgran# in the square. Worse than #gaol#. | If it was #good#. Mine went by ten, the year I left.

scene the_downs_at_home
when topic=land|travel|memories self.away=yes self.from=larkfield
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : I miss #downs#. You can watch the weather walk toward you from three hills off. | Sheep on #downs#, and the grass short as a carpet. You don't get that here. | Up on #downs# the wind dries your washing in an hour.
b : From three hills off? | No, you don't. | An hour!
a : Three. In the city it drops on you out of a chimney. | Here it's #cows# and wet grass and #hill# that won't stop. | An hour. Here it hangs a week and still smells of the Sound.

scene city_or_hill
when topic=travel|home here.region=ridgemere
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : I live in the city and work out on #hill#. Best of both, folk say. Worst of both, I say. | Every morning I walk out #lane# past the last houses, and I breathe easier. | The city has every sort of wolf and hardly a soul who knows a neighbour's #oldgran#.
b : Worst of both? | Easier? | Does that matter so much?
a : City rent, country hours. Somebody did well out of that, and it wasn't me. | The air's cleaner past the wall. And nobody's elbows, #address#. | It matters to me. You can't trust what you can't place.

scene how_i_talk
when topic=accent self.away=yes
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : They laugh when I say #gran# instead of grandmother. Let them. | I still price everything in my head the way they do at #cross#. City folk think I'm haggling. | Folk here say "a pound of butter". At home it's "#pound#". There's a difference.
b : It's a nice word. | Are you? | What's the difference?
a : It's the right word. Grandmother's for letters, #address#. | Only when they deserve it. | One's what they hand you. The other's what you're owed.

scene young_one_in_service
when topic=dreams|family
does tell.plan / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : I'd want one of the young ones of our house taken into service with a House. A proper place. | If I could have one thing, it'd be a #good# place for a young one. Up #hill#, in livery. | Service with a House is a life. Better than #churn#, and drier.
b : Which House? | Livery? | Is it really better?
a : Any of the four that'd have them. I'd not be choosy. | Livery, and a bed that's not damp, and my #gran#'s pride in it, were she living. | Drier, at least. I'll take drier, #address#.
b? : #bc_i_hope_so# | #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_maybe#

scene restday_quiet
when topic=faith|day here.day=rest
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Restday. I go to the chapel, I sharpen #blade#, I sleep. Nothing more to it. | I thank the Bright One on Restday for the butter coming. I ask for nothing else. | A quiet Restday, and nobody wanting #pound#. I'd not trade it.
b : Sounds peaceful. | Just that? | Nor would I.
a : Peaceful till somebody knocks. Don't be that somebody. | Just that. I'm not greedy, and #cream# keeps a day. | Well, then. We understand each other, #address#.

scene arms_and_shoulders
when topic=health
does tell.self / react.self / reassure
a : #shoulders.capitalize# are knots from the churn. They creak going up #hill#. | I've a bad wrist this week. Turned it wrong on a stiff batch. | I've not been #poorly# a day since I came here. Too #tired# to be sick.
b : You should rest it. | Bind it, then. | That's lucky.
a : Rest it and the butter doesn't come. Butter wins. | I have. A bit of rag and some patience. It'll mend, #address#. | Lucky, or stubborn. My #gran# would say both.

scene trusted_friend
when topic=friends self.friend
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I'd trust #self.friend# with #print#. That's more than I'd say of most. | If the roof came down on me, it's #self.friend# I'd send for. | I don't make friends quick. #self.friend# was worth the wait, #address#.
b : That's high trust. | Good to have. | Most are.
a : The highest I've got. | It is. One's enough, if it's the right one. | Not all. That one was. #bc_true#

scene the_neighbour
when topic=home|friends self.neighbour
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : #self.neighbour# keeps #step#. I notice a thing like that. | I'd wager #self.neighbour# had #grade#, without ever asking. | I'm glad of #self.neighbour# next door. Quiet, and no nose in my business. That's a gift.
b : You notice everything. | Without asking? | Rare, that.
a : Steps and grans. Not everything. | You can tell. It's in the manners, #address#. | Rare in the city. At home everybody's in your churn.

scene held_tongue
when topic=gossip|crime
does tell.gossip / react.news / insist / ack
a : I hear things off #milkcart#. I keep them. | Folk say plenty over #pound#. It goes no further than me. | There's talk going round. I'll not be the one carrying it, #address#.
b : Go on, tell me. | You must hear a lot. | Not even a hint?
a : No. It'll keep, and so will I. | A lot. And I'm #crock# with the lid on. Nothing pours out. | Not a hint. You'd only tell your #oldgran#, and she'd tell the street.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_fair_enough# | Ha. Fair.

scene theft_talk
when topic=gossip|crime topic.claim_kind=theft
does tell.gossip / react.news / assess
a : They're saying #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I'd not want to be #topic.their# #oldgran# today. | Word off #milkcart# is #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. That's a short pound of a wolf. | #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#, they say. I only heard it. I'd not put my print on it.
b : If it's true. | Hard words. | Nor would I.
a : If. Somebody's #oldgran# will know the truth of it. | Hard, but somebody has to say it, #address#. | Best not. #hearsay.capitalize#.

scene wedding_talk
when topic=gossip|news topic.claim_kind=marriage
does tell.news / react.news / assess
a : #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#. Two grans at one table. I'd pay to watch. | A wedding, then. #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#. #gladnews#. | They say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. Somebody'll want #crock# for the feast.
b : Two grans! | Good news. | You'll sell some, then.
a : Two grans, and only one can be right. #bc_ha# | Good. There's #little# enough of it. | Twenty pounds, if it's a proper one. #good.capitalize#.

scene new_faces
when topic=newcomer|player
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : There's a new face about. I'll know what to make of it when I've heard about the #oldgran#. | New folk come out #lane# asking for work. I send them to #boss# and keep my mouth shut. | A traveller wanted the road off me. I gave it, and I go no further with #stranger#.
b : You'll wait and see? | Wise. | That's fair.
a : I'll wait and see. #cream.capitalize# doesn't come for hurrying. | Wise or #tired#. Both, mostly. | Fair, and short. Short's best with strangers.

scene lean_half_pounds
when topic=times|prices world.times=hard|lean
does tell.worry / react.complaint / reassure / ack
a : Folk want half-pounds now. Nobody can run to #pound#. | #dairy.capitalize#'s cut a churn. I'm turning less and getting less. | Butter's a thing people stop buying first. I see the times in the orders off #milkcart#.
b : Hard for everyone. | Less pay? | What do they buy instead?
a : Hard. It'll turn. Times do, like #cream#. | Less #wages#, same rent. #oath#. | Lard. Or nothing. Nothing, mostly.
b? : #bc_true# | #bc_i_hope_so# | #bc_poor_soul#

scene full_orders
when topic=times|prices world.times=good|boom
does tell.news / react.news / assess
a : The orders are heavy this month. Every cook up the Heights wants #crock#. | Four churns a day and still behind. #busy.capitalize#. | Coin about. Folk are buying #pound# and asking for the print.
b : Good for the dairy. | Still behind? | For the print?
a : Good for #dairy#. #shoulders.capitalize# aren't so sure. | Behind, and glad of it, mostly. | They know it's honest weight. #print.capitalize#'s worth a penny on the price.

story nan_taught_me [topic=memories|family self.age>=25 self.from=larkfield]
  : My #gran# put me on a stool at her churn when I was a #child#, and told me to count.
  : Three hundred strokes. I cried at a hundred. She said #cream# doesn't care if you cry.
  : At two hundred and ninety it came. I've never forgotten the sound.
  ask : Can you still hear whoever taught you your first work, when you were small?
  b[old] : Somebody long gone, like yours. I still hear them.
  b : Nobody so patient. I picked things up as I went.

story the_batch_that_never_came [topic=work|memories self.age>=25]
  : Once I turned #churn# from dawn till dark and the butter never came. Not a fleck.
  : An old dairyman looked in, put his paw on the side, and said #cream# was too young.
  : He was right. Next morning it came in forty strokes. I've waited a day on cream ever since.
  b : Too young! What a thing to know.
  b[old] : The old ones always know. That's what they're for.

story gran_on_the_green [topic=pastimes|festival|memories self.age>=20 self.from=larkfield]
  : At a fair at home there was a big lump of a drover, threw everybody who came.
  : Then an old #oldgran# stepped onto the green, sleeves up, and asked him to try her.
  : She had him on his back in a breath. Turned out she'd been the champion when she was young.
  ask : Would you have stepped on the green against her?
  b : Never, not for any money. Good for her.
  b[old] : I'd have known better. The old ones keep their tricks.

story the_short_pound [topic=lore|prices|memories self.from=larkfield]
  : There was a seller at #cross# once who hid a stone inside his butter. Wrapped it neat as a gift.
  : A #oldgran# bought it, took it home, and cut it open for her #supper#.
  : She walked back with the stone in her paw and set it on the town's scales for all to see.
  : He never sold at #cross# again.
  b : Serves him right!
  b[job=merchant] : I'd have run him out myself. One like that spoils it for all of us.

story first_city_winter [topic=memories|travel|weather self.away=yes here.region=ridgemere]
  : My first winter here, #cream# froze on #milkcart# on the way down #hill#.
  : I carried #crock# into the dairy one at a time, under my coat, to thaw them.
  : Half the city got its butter late that week. Nobody said #thanks#.
  ask : Have you had a winter like that, where nothing would go right?
  b : Once or twice. They pass.
  b[from=larkfield] : The cold here's a wet cold. Not like at home.

rule @churning
when concept=bark working=yes self.job=none
does aside
cooldown 6h
say Come on, then. Come. #oath#. | Two hundred and ten. Two hundred and eleven. Come on, #cream#.

rule @rats_bark
when concept=bark working=yes self.dislikes=rats
does aside
cooldown 10h
say Get out of it. Out of #cream#. | #ratword.capitalize# again. I'll have a cat in, see if I don't.

rule @knife_bark
when concept=bark working=yes self.likes=sharp_knife
does aside
cooldown 10h
say Who's had #blade#? Somebody's dulled it. | Nobody touches #blade#. Nobody.

rule @dawn_bark
when concept=bark here.time=dawn
does aside
cooldown 8h
say #cream.capitalize# is cold as a well. | Up before #cows# again.

rule @mud_bark
when concept=bark here.weather=rain|storm self.dislikes=deep_mud
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Look at #lane#. #yardmud.capitalize#, the whole length. | Somebody lay a plank, for pity's sake. #annoyed#.

rule @warm_rain_bark
when concept=bark here.weather=rain here.season=spring|summer
does aside
cooldown 12h
say There. #softrain.capitalize#. Let it come. | Warm #rain#. The grass'll thank it.

dlg @1 resident.hub : #greet#. What is it? | #greet#. Quick, if you please, #address#.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #greet#, #topic.player#. I've not much time. What's wanted? | #topic.player#. You again. #go_ahead#.
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : It's late, and I'm up before #cows#. Be quick.
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : I've no notion who you are. Out with your business, then on your way. | #greet#. Say it short. I've work waiting, #address#.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.working=yes self.job=none self.workplace] : I churn the butter out at #self.workplace#. Pounds, printed and salted. That's my day. | I turn #churn# and wash the butter and press #print# on it. Not much to tell.
dlg @2 resident.job : I work. I keep to myself. That's about the size of it. | #job_busy# Mostly I'm #tired#.
dlg @1 resident.news [!world.news !world.event_words world.wagons!=raided world.death!=recent world.wedding!=recent] : #news_none# Ask #milkcart#. | Nothing comes out #lane# worth repeating.
dlg @2 resident.news [world.news] : Word from #world.news_from#: #world.news#. That's what #milkcart# says. | The cart had it from #world.news_from#: #world.news#.
dlg @3 resident.news [world.event_words] : It's all #world.event_words#, if you believe #milkcart#. | Everyone's on about #world.event_words#. I'm not.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip!=yes] : #gossip_none# | I'm #crock# with the lid tied down. Nothing pours out of me.
dlg @2 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes] : They say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I'd not swear to it. | Folk say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. #hearsay.capitalize#, mind.
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. | #farewell#. Mind #yardmud#.
dlg @1 master.learn [here.teaches=yes] : You want to learn #churn#? It's arms and patience. I'll teach the arms; the patience is yours to find. | #learn_offer# You'll want strong shoulders and no hurry in you.
dlg @2 master.learn [here.teaches=yes topic.meeting=known] : Back for another go at #churn#, #topic.player#? Roll your sleeves up. | #topic.player#. Your strokes were uneven last time. We'll fix that.
dlg @3 master.learn [here.teaches=no] : I've a learner already, and one's my limit. Ask me what you like, though. | I can't take another on. I'll answer a question or two about #churn#.
dlg @1 master.work : Up before #cows#, three or four churns a day, and the washing after. It's not pretty. | Turning, washing, salting, patting. And watching for #ratword#.
dlg @1 master.wages : Nobody pays a learner. You turn beside me, you learn it quicker than alone, and what you make you sell. | No #wages# for learning. That's the old way and the fair one.
dlg @1 master.takes [here.take_on=yes] : One learner at a time, and one I trust. You'd do. Ask, and it's settled. | Strong arms and a still tongue. You've the one; I'll take your word on the other.
dlg @2 master.takes [here.take_on=no] : One at a time, and one I know. #here.take_on_why# | Not now. #here.take_on_why#
dlg @1 master.bye : #farewell#. Come back with your sleeves rolled. | #farewell#. Mind #shoulders#.
graft resident.hub
choice What would you make of my grandmother? -> gran
node gran
say Your gran? I'd want to hear how she kept her step and how she salted her words. A gran who keeps a firm step raises a wolf you can trust with a crock.
mood calm
free
choice And what about yours? -> gran_mine
choice Goodbye. -> bye
node gran_mine
say Mine? First weight, and never a short pound in her life. I learned the churn on her stool. More than that, you'll not have from me.
choice Something else. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye

graft master.learn
choice How do you know when the butter's ready? -> come
node come
say You hear it before you see it. The slosh goes thick and slapping, and the handle fights you. Stop then, or it turns to grease.
mood calm
free
choice What happens after? -> come_after
choice Back to the trade. -> learn
node come_after
say Drain the buttermilk, wash the butter in brine till the water runs clear, salt it, pat it, and press the print on top. Then you've a pound you can sell.
choice Back to the trade. -> learn
choice Goodbye. -> bye
